      "block_id": "BL-6137-2.6.06-02",
      "block_type": "worked_application",
      "heading": "",
      "text": "Government establishes and implements the relevant conservation framework, manages public responsibilities and supports enforcement or monitoring. Rules can protect species, regulate use and guide management. Their effectiveness depends on suitable ecological information, practical capacity and compliance. A rule on paper does not demonstrate that illegal removal has fallen or that habitat condition improved. Patrol, incident and resource records help assess outcomes while recognising reporting limitations. In Namibia the government passed the Nature Conservation Amendment Act of 1996, which let communal residents gain rights over wildlife and tourism by registering conservancies. It runs national parks such as Etosha, works with conservancies and NGOs on annual game counts and issues hunting and use quotas based on them, and enforces the law against poaching. The results are largely successful but not complete: elephant numbers rose from about 7,600 in 1995 to about 23,600 in 2016, yet since 2012 counts of several species in north-western monitoring areas have fallen because of drought, animal movement and suspected poaching.",
