    {
      "block_id": "BL-6137-2.6.06-07",
      "block_type": "plain_explanation",
      "heading": "",
      "text": "Private enterprises can provide capital, operating skills, employment and payments through tourism or other appropriately regulated wildlife activities. Demand can give an economic reason to maintain habitat or wildlife. The incentive depends on actual returns, obligations and control. An enterprise can also create disturbance, waste or unequal benefits if poorly managed. Profitability and sustainability therefore need separate evidence rather than one being assumed from the other. In 2022 conservancies held 67 joint-venture tourism agreements with private lodge operators and 44 conservation hunting concessions; operators pay fees or a share of income to the conservancy, and hunting income helps pay for anti-poaching work and game guards.",
