      "block_type": "plain_explanation",
      "heading": "",
      "text": "Communities bring local knowledge, rights, labour and direct experience of wildlife benefits and costs. Communal conservancies provide a Namibian framework for member participation and management. Ministry information describes elected committees and reporting responsibilities. These arrangements create opportunities for accountability, while their actual operation must be assessed through participation, understandable information and delivery. A committee's existence does not establish every member's agreement. Conservancy members also protect wildlife directly: in 2022 conservancies employed 733 community game guards, who monitor animals and report poaching, and members take part in annual game counts and decide how income and game meat are shared.",
      "claim_ids": [
        "CL-6137-2.6.06-1",
        "CL-6137-2.6.06-2",
        "CL-6137-2.6.06-01-1",
