    {
      "block_id": "BL-6137-2.6.06-21",
      "block_type": "plain_explanation",
      "heading": "",
      "text": "A community survey can reveal views but requires a suitable target population and neutral questions. Random, systematic and stratified selection have different coverage implications. A conveniently available group at a meeting may omit people who could not attend. A majority among those interviewed is evidence about that sample, not proof of unanimous community consent. The limitation should guide a suitable improvement, such as broader eligible coverage or clearer access to participation.",
